  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 248 AM EDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nRain showers have mostly dwindled away and have become relegated to \nthe southern half of the U.P. early this morning as the first \nshortwave has now passed through the area. While not much \nprecipitation has been seen over the past 24 hours, enough has \nfallen to potentially produce areas of fog across the land before \ndawn this morning. However, a 'wrench' in the fog prediction is the \nupper-level cloud cover overhead; this cloud cover will actually \nlimit fog development as it will weaken the inversion just above the \nsfc. Regardless, some spots could get down to quarter mile or less \nvisibilities. Should this occur, then an SPS or Dense Fog Advisory \ncould be forthcoming. As the sun rises, expect the fog to slowly \ndwindle away, with the U.P. becoming fog-free by the mid to late \nmorning hours. \n\nA second shortwave looks to move through the Upper Midwest today, \nwith maybe some showers and weak thunderstorms moving through the \narea today. However, with the shortwave moving through south of the \narea (i.e. Wisconsin and central Lake MI), rain chances seem fairly \nlimited, ranging from around 10% in the Keweenaw and in Luce County \nall the way up to 30/35% in the interior west and south central. \nBehind this shortwave, additional fog could form across the region \ntonight.  \n\nThings become more interesting during the overnight hours as a warm \nfront 24-hours ago looking to come during the day on Monday is now \nslated to arrive earlier, potentially bringing heavy rain showers \nand thunderstorms back across the U.P. during the overnight hours. \nBecause the warm front now looks to be coming in during the \novernight hours, expect the severe weather threat to now be very low \nif not next to zero as instability will be limited to above the sfc \ninversion and the mid-levels. That being said, won't completely rule \nout a stronger thunderstorm or two where some sub-severe/non-\ndamaging hail and gusty winds could be seen. In addition, some spots \nmay actually see a wetting to even soaking rainfall from this round \nof convection, as the latest HREF shows a 30 to 85% chance for at \nleast a quarter inch of rainfall with the passage of the warm front \ntonight through Monday; there is even up to a 60% chance for some \nportions of the interior west to see over an inch of rain (including \nIronwood). The rainfall would also bring an end to the fog threat \novernight, although fog could reform before dawn Monday morning once \nthe rain showers and storms cease across any particular area. That \nbeing said, as the warm front continues NE late tonight through \nMonday, CAMs show the line of convection along the warm front \nbreaking up and weakening with time during the early morning hours \non Monday. While a second round of convection may occur via a \nsecondary shortwave moving through behind the warm front Monday \nafternoon, expect this round of precipitation to be rather quick, if \nit even moves through at all. Thus, not as much rainfall is expected \nover the more NE areas of the U.P. late tonight through Monday, and \nflash flooding is not expected given the very dry ground. \n\nExpect rain chances to in general decrease for the remainder of the \nforecast period as high pressure ridging builds back over the \ncentral CONUS and into the Great Lakes region. That being said, the \nlatest runs of the medium range guidance do suggest that a shortwave \nrotating on the southern end of the ridging could move through the \nUpper Great Lakes this Wednesday. If the track of the shortwave is \nclose enough to the U.P., some additional (much-needed) showers and \nthunderstorms could be seen across the area. Otherwise, expect \nmainly dry weather, although the above normal temperatures and humid \nconditions seen today look to continue into early September as Gulf \nair continues to be funneled into the region.",
